\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Feedback, reward, credit, and update}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{feedback}
\tag{reward}
\tag{optimization}

\p{A completed rollout produces an immutable \strong{rollout record}}

##{
  \rho=(q,\nu_b,\tau,y,e,c),
}

\p{where #{q} is the task, #{\nu_b} identifies the behaviour-policy version,
#{\tau} is the public trajectory, #{y} is the terminal outcome, #{e} is
verifier or environment evidence, and #{c} is the realized cost vector. A
\strong{feedback operator} maps causally available fields of #{\rho} to a
typed signal. A \strong{reward} is a scalar component of that signal; feedback
may instead contain preferences, process labels, tests, critiques, latent
targets, or structured verifier traces.}

\p{A \strong{credit rule} assigns the realized signal to actions, tokens,
turns, or trajectory summaries. An \strong{update proposal} combines credited
records with the current parameters and optimizer state to produce a candidate
parameter and state transition. Acceptance, clipping, or gating is a separate
operation; a rejected proposal does not retroactively change the rollout
record.}

\p{For reuse, let #{\mathcal B_t} be a persistent replay pool of immutable
records. A selection operator evaluated against the current policy produces
attempt-local annotations}

##{
  \operatorname{Select}(\theta_t,\mathcal B_t)
  =(\rho, r_t, a_t, k_t, m_t),
}

\p{where #{r_t} denotes current-to-behaviour ratios, #{a_t} record age,
#{k_t} reuse count, and #{m_t} clipping or mask status. Credit, proposal, and
gate consume this attempt data; they do not store mutable reuse state inside
#{\rho}. This separation makes fresh generation, replay, and gradient gating
different protocol choices over the same evidence.}

\p{Supervised next-token loss, preference loss, outcome reward, process reward,
and representation-targeted auxiliary loss are therefore different feedback
and objective interfaces. They may be combined, but their semantics and costs
must remain named.}
